## Summary

jsoup resolves a charset conflict inside `DataUtil.detectCharset(...)` in `src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java`, using a strict priority order: **byte‑order‑mark (BOM) > externally‑supplied charset (HTTP `Content-Type` header or caller‑specified charset) > in‑document declared charset (`<meta charset>`/`<meta http-equiv>`/XML declaration)** — and only the last case can trigger a full re‑parse.

## How the conflict is detected and resolved

**1. BOM always wins, and overrides any externally supplied charset**

At the start of `detectCharset`, a BOM is checked first, before anything else is considered:

```java
String bomCharset = detectCharsetFromBom(input); // resets / consumes appropriately
if (bomCharset != null)
    charsetName = bomCharset;
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java`, method `detectCharset`)

This unconditionally overwrites whatever `charsetName` was passed in — including a charset that came from an HTTP `Content-Type` header (set in `HttpConnection.Response.execute` via `res.charset = DataUtil.getCharsetFromContentType(res.contentType());` in `src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/HttpConnection.java`) or from a caller-supplied argument to `Jsoup.parse(...)`.

**2. If an explicit charset is still set (non‑null) after the BOM check, jsoup does *not* look at the document's own declared charset at all:**

```java
} else { // specified by content type header (or by user on file load)
    Validate.notEmpty(charsetName, "Must set charset arg to character set of file to parse. Set to null to attempt to detect from HTML");
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java`, method `detectCharset`)

In this branch there is no scanning for `<meta charset>` etc., and no re-parse is triggered — the externally supplied charset simply wins, and any conflicting `<meta charset>` in the document is ignored for decoding purposes.

**3. Only when no charset was supplied at all (`charsetName == null`) does jsoup sniff the document, and only then can a conflict trigger a re-parse:**

```java
if (charsetName == null) { // read ahead and determine from meta. safe first parse as UTF-8
    ...
    try (Reader reader = new SimpleStreamReader(input, UTF_8)) {
        doc = parser.parseInput(reader, baseUri);
        ...
    }
    // look for <meta http-equiv="Content-Type" ...> or HTML5 <meta charset="...">
    Elements metaElements = doc.select(metaCharset);
    ...
    // look for <?xml encoding='ISO-8859-1'?>
    ...
    foundCharset = validateCharset(foundCharset);
    if (foundCharset != null && !foundCharset.equalsIgnoreCase(defaultCharsetName)) { // need to re-decode
        foundCharset = foundCharset.trim().replaceAll("[\"']", "");
        charsetName = foundCharset;
        doc = null;
    } else if (reuseDocIfFullyRead && input.baseReadFully()) {
        input.close();
    } else {
        doc = null;
    }
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java`, method `detectCharset`)

Here the document is first speculatively parsed as UTF-8 (the "default"). If a `<meta charset>`/`<meta http-equiv=content-type>` tag or an XML declaration's `encoding` attribute is found, and that declared value differs from UTF-8 (`!foundCharset.equalsIgnoreCase(defaultCharsetName)`), that is treated as a genuine conflict with the speculative UTF-8 parse: `charsetName` is updated to the found value and `doc` is set to `null`.

**4. The re-parse itself happens in `parseInputStream`:**

```java
static Document parseInputStream(CharsetDoc charsetDoc, String baseUri, Parser parser) throws IOException {
    // if doc != null it was fully parsed during charset detection; so just return that
    if (charsetDoc.doc != null)
        return charsetDoc.doc;
    ...
    try (Reader reader = new SimpleStreamReader(input, charset)) {
        doc = parser.parseInput(reader, baseUri);
        ...
    }
    return doc;
}
```
(`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java`, method `parseInputStream`)

Because `charsetDoc.doc` was set to `null` by the conflict branch above, this method does *not* reuse the speculative UTF-8-parsed document; instead it re-reads the (rewound, since `input.mark`/`input.reset` were used) input stream using the newly discovered `charset` and produces a fresh `Document`.

## What triggers the re-parse, precisely

The re-parse trigger is the boolean condition:

```java
foundCharset != null && !foundCharset.equalsIgnoreCase(defaultCharsetName)
```

in `DataUtil.detectCharset` (`src/main/java/org/jsoup/helper/DataUtil.java`) — i.e., a declared in-document charset (from `<meta charset>`, `<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="...charset=...">`, or an `<?xml encoding=...?>` declaration/processing instruction) that is not UTF-8 (`defaultCharsetName`), found only in the scenario where **no charset was already known** from a BOM or from an external caller/HTTP source. This sets `doc = null`, which forces `parseInputStream` to re-decode and re-parse the buffered input using the document’s own declared charset rather than reusing the initial UTF-8 parse.

If, on the other hand, a charset was already known (BOM, or explicitly from the caller/HTTP `Content-Type`), that branch is skipped entirely (`Validate.notEmpty(charsetName, ...)` branch), so a conflicting in-document `<meta charset>` value is never consulted and no re-parse occurs — the external/BOM-declared charset simply takes precedence.
